        title: 'The Man Who Flew In On Thursday',
        body: 'The building committee had nine months of meetings behind them and a roof that leaked over the choir loft, and somebody knew somebody, and so they hired the man who flew in on Thursday. He was good. Nobody argued that. He had a deck with forty-one slides and the third one had a photograph of their own sanctuary on it, which impressed everybody, and he used the word sequencing eleven times in ninety minutes. He had a recommendation: re-roof in two phases, defer the loft, and move the kitchen renovation ahead of the HVAC because of a lead-time thing. The committee voted it through that night, because it was the first time in nine months anyone had told them what to do. Then he flew out Thursday evening. What nobody on the committee could have told you, because nobody asked and he never said, was that he had been in the building for four hours and had not been upstairs. The loft he recommended deferring is where the water comes in, and it comes in over the panel box. Brother Alton knew that. Brother Alton is a retired electrician who had been there every Saturday for six years, who had put a five-gallon bucket under that spot himself, and who had not been invited to the Thursday meeting because he was not on the committee. He mentioned the panel box to the chairman in the parking lot afterward, and the chairman said he would raise it, and he did raise it, three weeks later, and by then the phased re-roof was signed. The kitchen is beautiful now. The panel box was replaced in February, by emergency, at four times the number on slide twenty-nine, and the loft is still roped off. The consultant was not a liar and he was not lazy. He was simply not there. Four hours is not a week, and a photograph of a sanctuary is not a sanctuary, and an answer given before the matter has been heard is folly no matter how well it is formatted, "He that answereth a matter before he heareth it, it is folly and shame unto him" (Proverbs 18:13). The committee did not buy bad advice. They bought advice from a distance, and they paid the difference.',
