        body: 'The food pantry was failing and everybody had a theory. The denomination sent Maricel, who was twenty-six, had a degree in operations, and had been warned by three people on the drive down that the director was proud and would not listen. She arrived on a Monday. She did not open her laptop. She asked where the boxes went and then she carried boxes, and on Tuesday she carried boxes, and on Wednesday she stood in the line outside in the cold with a clipboard she never wrote on because she wanted to know how long the line actually was, which turned out to be fifty minutes, not the twenty everybody said. Thursday she rode the truck. Friday she washed the bins. Saturday she sat at the folding table while the volunteers argued about the sign-in sheet and she said nothing at all, and Sunday she went to their church and sat in the back. On the eighth day the director asked her, a little sharply, what the denomination had sent her to tell him. She said one sentence. She said the bottleneck is not the food and it is not the volunteers, it is that the sign-in table and the pickup table are the same table, so every family waits twice, and if you move the sign-in to the sidewalk under the awning the line is twenty minutes. He stared at her. Then he said, nobody has ever said anything to me that was about my actual building. They moved the table that afternoon. Here is the part that is not about tables. Maricel did not earn the right to be heard by being right; she was right BECAUSE she had been there, and the seven days were not patience as a technique, they were the only way the fifty minutes could have been known. There is a prophet who did this. He was sent to the hardest audience in the Word and before he said one syllable, "Then I came to them of the captivity at Telabib, that dwelt by the river of Chebar, and I sat where they sat, and remained there astonished among them seven days" (Ezekiel 3:15). Seven days, inside it, astonished. And only then, "And it came to pass at the end of seven days, that the word of the LORD came unto me, saying," (Ezekiel 3:16). The nearness came first. The commission came second. That order is not a management technique, it is Yahweh’s own order, and it has been in print for about twenty-six centuries.',
