    title: 'Sat where they sat — the oldest forward-deployed pattern, and the video we never saw',
    bigIdea:
      'WHERE THIS CAME FROM, AT ITS REAL SIZE. Darrell forwarded a McKinsey & Company email with two words typed above the header, and they are two instructions rather than a label: Lesson and research. The subject was What is a forward-deployed engineer? and here is the thing a reader must know before anything else on this page: THE SEND IS A PROMOTION FOR A VIDEO, NOT AN ARTICLE. It carries no article, no byline, no study and no reporting — two teaser paragraphs, three headshots, three names, one link to a video Explainer, and then subscription furniture. WE NEVER SAW THE VIDEO. Nothing on this page tells you what the video said, because nothing on this page could. Everything taught below is either the Word, which we quote verbatim from the corpus in this app, or the two sentences the email itself contains, which we quote as the publication’s words and never as ours. Nobody taught this lesson. There was no class and no recording; an A.I. agent drafted it from a forwarded promotion and the gates decide whether it stands. The three people the email names — Holger Hürtgen, Martin Harrysson and Nick Robson — are named in the SOURCE as the speakers in a video, not as teachers of this lesson, and not one view is attributed to any of them beyond the sentences printed here. THE ADVERTISING FURNITURE WAS IGNORED, which is the standing rule: app-store badges, Follow our thinking, share buttons, manage-subscriptions and unsubscribe links are not the lesson and are not taught. One line of it is reported as a measured fact rather than a complaint, because it is unusual to see it printed so plainly: the email states, word for word: By opening our emails or clicking on links, you agree to our use of cookies and web tracking technology. Opening a message is treated as consent. That is worth a believer knowing, and it is not what this lesson is about. ' +
      'THREE TIERS, SO A TRUTH IS NOT SHRUNK AND A CLAIM IS NOT INFLATED. ESTABLISHED, measured from the message in hand: it is a promotion for a video; it carries no article and no byline; and the publication PRINTS ITS OWN SCEPTICISM ABOUT ITS OWN COVER STORY, which is the most interesting line in it — the term, word for word from the send: may be a buzzword for a service that consulting firms and technology providers have offered for years: embedding engineers at a client’s company, inside its systems and data, to build software alongside the people who will use it. It also states that the three speakers’ views on the separation between an FDE and a traditional consultant suggest the role is still evolving, word for word — which is a careful way of saying the three do not agree. A publication saying its own cover story may be an old service with a new name is telling the truth, and we state it plainly rather than hedging it: the publisher said it, in print, about itself. ATTRIBUTED, their words and never asserted on our authority, word for word: Interest in forward-deployed engineers, or FDEs, has surged in recent months as companies try to get real value from AI. There is no number behind that sentence anywhere in the send — no survey, no sample, no date range, no definition of interest. It is a trend claim with nothing under it, so it stays theirs. Likewise theirs: that the three agree that FDEs can help companies derive value from AI by tailoring it to a company’s specific problems, word for word — which is the email’s summary of a video we did not watch. UNVERIFIED, and therefore not taught as fact: when the role began or who began it; whether the originating company’s own filings or careers pages use the term; and anything whatsoever that the video says beyond the two sentences above. OVER-REACH, which the Word corrects while leaving the real practice standing: the implication that nearness to the work is a NEW thing invented for the age of A.I. It is not. "The thing that hath been, it is that which shall be; and that which is done is that which shall be done: and there is no new thing under the sun" (Ecclesiastes 1:9). The practice is genuinely good — being inside the work really does produce better counsel — and the Word is the oldest witness that it does. ' +
      'TRUST BUT VERIFY — WHAT WE CHECKED, AND WHAT THE CHECKING CHANGED. What we checked: the send’s own claim that this is a service offered for years. The primary source for that question is the originating company’s own public writing on the role, and the second primary source is its own filed prospectus, because a filing is dated and legally attested. Both roads were refused by this session’s network. www.palantir.com returned the refusal getaddrinfo ENOTFOUND and so did www.sec.gov and sec.gov; the full-text search host returned a proxy refusal rather than a page. The publication’s own Explainer page, which is the only road to the video’s substance, returned the refusal getaddrinfo ENOTFOUND for www.mckinsey.com as well. SO SAY IT PLAINLY: NO PRIMARY SOURCE WAS READ. The only road that worked was a search index, which returns other people’s summaries, not primary documents, and what it returned disagreed with itself — two different years are circulated for when the role began, and neither could be confirmed. WHAT THE CHECKING CHANGED, and this is the honest part: before checking, this lesson was going to date the practice and call the title a rename of a decades-old service. After checking, it dates nothing. The claim that it is an old service is carried as the PUBLICATION’S OWN PRINTED WORDS, stated plainly because they printed it, and the date is dropped entirely rather than guessed. The honest limit: the video is unwatched, the filings are unread, and no teaching on this page rests on either. ' +
      'AND NOW THE PART THAT DOES NOT DEPEND ON ANY OF THAT. The idea being sold has a case in the Word so much older and so much better that the newsletter’s question answers itself. A prophet sat where they sat for seven days before he was given a word. A cupbearer walked the broken wall at night and told nobody until he had seen it. A father-in-law watched a whole day before he opened his mouth. An apostle made tents in the same shop as the people he taught. And the Word Himself did not send counsel; He came. That is the lesson.',
    inApp:
      'Take one real thing you are advising on or being advised about right now — a church building or renovation, a rehab, a property turn, a ministry launch, a vendor you are about to sign — and answer three questions in writing. ONE, the nearness question: how many hours has the person giving the advice actually spent inside the work, not in a meeting about the work? Write the number. If you do not know it, that is the answer. TWO, the hireling question: whose own are the sheep? Write down what it costs the adviser if the recommendation is wrong, in their own money, their own name, or their own Saturday. If the answer is nothing, the counsel is not worthless but it is unsecured, and you price it accordingly, because "The hireling fleeth, because he is an hireling, and careth not for the sheep" (John 10:13). THREE, the Brother Alton question: who has been inside this work for years and has not been asked? Write the name. Then ask them this week, before the next decision, and write down what they say even if it inconveniences the plan. In the app, open your Projects page and keep the three answers as a note on the project itself, so the record lives where the work does; and if you are the one being sent in, write the date you will have finished watching and the date you will first speak, and keep them in that order. Parents, do this with your children on something small they are part of, and let them write the third name.',
