        title: 'The Two Stones',
        body: 'A grain seller kept two stones. The heavier one he used when farmers brought grain in, so that a sack weighed a little short and he paid a little less. The lighter one he used when townspeople came to buy, so that a sack weighed a little long and they carried away a little less. Neither stone was chipped or false. Each was exactly what it was. He simply kept one in the bag at his belt and one on the shelf behind him, and he never used both in the same hour.\n\nFor eleven years nobody caught him, and he came to believe the arrangement was safe because it was careful. What he had not accounted for was a widow who both grew grain and bought flour. She was not watching him. She had no theory about him. She simply came in one season carrying and another season buying, and one morning she said, without any edge in her voice, that his sacks seemed to change their minds.\n\nHe had an answer ready, because he had always had an answer ready. But the answer only explained one of the stones, and she had met both. By the next market day three farmers were weighing their own sacks at home before they brought them, which is a thing nobody does unless they have stopped believing you. He lost the eleven years in a week, and not because the widow accused him of anything. She never did. She only ever said the sacks seemed to change their minds, and everyone who heard it understood it at once, because most of them had met both stones too and had never had cause to put the two encounters side by side.',
