        title: 'The Second List',
        body: 'A builder ran two crews on one estate, and he wrote the rule for both on a board in the site office: no trench left open past the end of the day. The first crew read the board each morning. The second crew worked the far end and rarely came to the office, so their foreman copied the rules onto a card and carried it in his pocket, which seemed to everybody like diligence.\n\nIn the spring the builder changed the rule - trenches could stay open where they were fenced and lit, because the ground was taking too long to reopen. He changed the board. He told the crew in front of him. Nobody thought to go and find a card in a pocket at the far end of the estate, because nobody had written down that a card existed.\n\nFor a month the two crews worked to two rules, and both were obeying what they had been given. The builder discovered it only because an inspector walked the whole site in one afternoon and asked, mildly, why the east end was being backfilled every evening and the west end was not.\n\nHe did not issue a corrected card. He took the card, and he put the board where both crews had to pass it, and he made the far foreman\'s morning start with reading it rather than remembering it. When the rule changed again that autumn, it changed once. The point was never that the card had been wrong. The card had been right on the day it was written, which is exactly what makes a second list dangerous: it does not go wrong, it goes out of date, and it does so silently, while everybody holding it believes they are being careful.',
