    bigIdea: 'WHO TAUGHT THIS: NOBODY. Gmail thread 1a17ed862dc396be - a Retail Dive send forwarded out of Darrell\'s university mailbox on 2026-10-27 at 19:31 UTC with one line typed above the forwarded header - Lesson and research and implementation of the research. THE SEND IS AN ADVERTISEMENT AND SAYS SO ITSELF, naming itself custom content by Studio created for a sponsor of the publication; the sponsor sells payment systems. ITS REPORT WAS NEVER READ - it sits behind a form demanding a real person\'s name, employer and job title, and we do not spend Darrell\'s details to fetch a vendor\'s PDF - so the survey of 100+ retail leaders it advertises is quoted nowhere, summarised nowhere, and leaned on nowhere below. Exactly ONE claim in the email stands without that report, and it is the lesson: customers expect one message across every channel, and when it is not there they NOTICE. THE SEAM WAS CHOSEN BY MEASURING WHAT THIS COURSE ALREADY TEACHES: pm14 covers WHICH measure, pm12 capability over titles, pm15 proximity to the work - so this lands on the SAME measure in every place, which none of them teaches. Deuteronomy 25:13-14 is the anchor and it is exact: not one bad weight but TWO, a great and a small, one in the bag and one in the house - so which measure you met depended on WHERE you met him. Put the modern word channel where the old word place stands and the ancient fraud is an ordinary modern company - the failure the industry calls a broken omnichannel experience and attacks with a single source of truth, which is precisely the repair this lesson lands on. THE HOUSE\'S OWN WITNESS, first-party and checkable rather than a vendor\'s survey: on the day this was written this platform was caught carrying two standards itself - the Love Corner\'s church door showed the platform\'s name in its persistent header, and on a phone its bottom bar offered four platform surfaces inside a church-branded app, because that bar had been added later as a fixed list and never learned the church-only rule the navigation code already enforced correctly. Nobody authored it; it accumulated. Fixed the same day by making the bar READ the one rule instead of keeping a second list.',
    inApp: 'No new screens this week. What changes is a page you write by hand, and it takes about an hour. FIRST, list every door a person can reach you through - not the ones you maintain, the ones that are open. The website, the app, the phone, the counter, the invoice, the email, and the human being who answers when somebody asks a question after church. The channel that eventually embarrasses you is dependably the one nobody wrote down, because nobody owned it. SECOND, write THE message once, in one place: the price, the promise, the terms, who you are. If it currently lives in four places it is already four messages that simply have not finished diverging. THIRD, walk every door yourself as a stranger would - in the real thing, not in the documentation. That is how this platform\'s own defect was found this week: not by reading the navigation code, which was correct throughout, but by opening the app on a phone and looking at what was actually on the screen. FOURTH, wherever two things must agree, make one of them READ the other rather than trusting a person to keep them in step, and where a machine can check the agreement, let it, so a drift fails loudly instead of quietly. Bring the list of doors to the next session. The one that cannot be made to read the others is the one to talk about.',
