      theme: 'THE SIN IS NOT A BAD MEASURE. THE SIN IS MORE THAN ONE.\n\nDeuteronomy 25:13-14 does not describe a merchant with a broken weight. It describes a merchant with TWO accurate ones - a great and a small - who selects. And it puts the second one in a different PLACE: one in thy bag, one in thine house. Which standard a person met was a question of where they met him. That is a channel problem, written down before there were channels.\n\nProverbs states it three times across two books (11:1, 20:10, 20:23), and the register is severe: abomination, set against a just weight that is His DELIGHT. The reward attached in Deuteronomy 25:15 is length of days - continuation, the relationship that goes on - which is precisely what the advertisement calls a returning customer.\n\nMatthew 5:37 puts the same standard on the mouth: one answer, repeated. James 1:8 names the cost to the person keeping two - unstable in ALL his ways, because two answers must be tracked, and the tracking fails publicly. 2 Corinthians 1:17-18 is Paul refusing exactly this charge and grounding his reliability in Yahweh\'s character rather than his own intentions; Hebrews 13:8 is that character. Psalms 15:4 is the expensive version - sweareth to his own hurt, and changeth not - without which consistency has only ever been convenient. Luke 16:10 closes the escape: the least and the much are the same thing.',
