      "theme": "THE AGREEMENT IS NOT THE LAST STEP. IT IS THE FIRST ONE.\n\nAmos asks it as a question with one obvious answer, and the obvious answer is the governing constraint on every project with two offices in it: two cannot walk together unless they are agreed. Not agreeable. Agreed. The verse does not ask whether they like each other, whether they report to the same person, or whether they have met. It asks whether the agreement exists, because walking together is downstream of it.\n\nThen the Word does what a management article cannot: it gives the worked case, with the officers named and the procedure written down. Exodus 38 puts the man who MADE the thing and the man who COUNTED the materials in the same passage, by name, each with his own office. 2 Kings 12 writes out an actual dual-control procedure over a chest of money - the king's scribe and the high priest counting it together - and then records the extraordinary consequence of that control: the workmen's accounts were not audited at all, because the men dealt faithfully. Acts 6 shows a complaint that was a NUMBERS complaint, answered not with a sermon but with an org chart and seven men of honest report. And 2 Corinthians 8 gives the reason the whole discipline exists, in the plainest sentence anybody has ever written about financial control: providing for honest things, not only in the sight of the Lord, but also in the sight of men.\n\nTwo offices, one house, one set of numbers, and the agreement standing in front of all of it. The industry's name for the gap is misalignment. The Word's name for the cure is older, and it is a question."
